<%#
  T-28 increment 3 — the form that makes a link.

  --- WHAT THIS FORM DELIBERATELY CANNOT EXPRESS ---

  There is no "never expires" and no way to leave the expiry blank: `expires_at` is NOT NULL
  in the schema and bounded in the model, because §7b.1's whole indictment of the base
  plugin starts with a token that never expires. A form that could ask for one would mean
  the rule lived in a validation rather than in the design.

  Redmine's own chrome only — `box tabular`, `p`, `.info`, `.warning`.
%>
<h2><%= l(:label_reporter_share_link_new) %></h2>

<p><%= link_to @template.name, project_reporter_template_path(@project, @template) %></p>

<%#
  SAID BEFORE THE FORM, NOT AFTER IT. What a share link actually does is the thing people
  get wrong about it, and by the time somebody has filled in an expiry they have already
  decided. Two sentences: the report is frozen now, as you; and whoever holds the URL sees
  it without signing in to anything.
%>
<div class="info">
  <p><%= l(:text_reporter_share_link_what_it_does) %></p>
</div>

<%= form_tag(project_reporter_template_share_links_path(@project, @template), method: :post) do %>
  <div class="box tabular">
    <p>
      <label for="purpose"><%= l(:field_reporter_share_link_purpose) %></label>
      <%= text_field_tag 'purpose', params[:purpose], size: 60, maxlength: 255 %>
      <em class="info"><%= l(:text_reporter_share_link_purpose_info) %></em>
    </p>

    <p>
      <label for="expires_in_days"><%= l(:field_reporter_share_link_expires_in_days) %></label>
      <%= number_field_tag 'expires_in_days',
                           params[:expires_in_days] || ReporterDashboards::ShareLinksController::DEFAULT_EXPIRY_DAYS,
                           min: 1,
                           max: ReporterDashboards::ShareLinksController::MAX_EXPIRY_DAYS %>
      <em class="info"><%= l(:text_reporter_share_link_expires_info,
                             max: ReporterDashboards::ShareLinksController::MAX_EXPIRY_DAYS) %></em>
    </p>

    <p>
      <label for="max_uses"><%= l(:field_reporter_share_link_max_uses) %></label>
      <%= number_field_tag 'max_uses', params[:max_uses], min: 1 %>
      <em class="info"><%= l(:text_reporter_share_link_max_uses_info) %></em>
    </p>

    <%#
      THE PUBLIC TICKBOX IS OFFERED ONLY TO SOMEBODY WHO HOLDS THE SECOND PERMISSION, and
      the controller checks it again per request — the form is not the check. FR-62 makes
      public links off by default, and T-28's `Accept:` makes them a SECOND decision rather
      than a stronger version of the first, so it is a separate field with its own warning
      rather than a dropdown of "visibility".
    %>
    <% if User.current.allowed_to?(:publish_reporter_dashboards_reports, @project) %>
      <p>
        <label for="public_link"><%= l(:field_reporter_share_link_public) %></label>
        <%= check_box_tag 'public_link', '1', params[:public_link].present? %>
        <em class="info"><%= l(:text_reporter_share_link_public_info) %></em>
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%#
    THE WARNING IS UNCONDITIONAL, because it is true of every link on this form and not only
    of a public one: whoever holds the URL sees the report. Showing it only next to the
    public tickbox would imply a private link is safe to forward, which is the exact
    misunderstanding this page exists to prevent.
  %>
  <div class="warning">
    <p><%= l(:text_reporter_share_link_warning) %></p>
  </div>

  <p>
    <%= submit_tag l(:button_reporter_share_link_create) %>
    <%= link_to l(:button_cancel),
                project_reporter_template_share_links_path(@project, @template) %>
  </p>
<% end %>

<% html_title l(:label_reporter_share_link_new) %>
